<a href="http://gangrey.com/610">
<h2 class="design">'A Very Blurry, Foggy Line'</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">Really?</h4>

<p>

Was in Pittsburgh last weekend and picked up a City Paper. The <a href="http://www.pittsburghcitypaper.ws/gyrobase/Content?oid=oid%3A19690">cover story</a> was called Nonfiction: Is literature erasing the distinction between fact and falsehood? An excerpt: Yet <a href="http://www.creativenonfiction.org/thejournal/gutkind.htm">Gutkind</a> sympathizes with <a href="http://www.slate.com/id/2134203/">Frey</a>, and not just because it was Frey's success that got him busted for crimes lesser-known writers have long gotten away with. Gutkind contends that the truth of an event -- not to mention the memory of it -- can itself be subjective. "It's a very blurry, foggy line" between what's allowed and what's prohibited in creative nonfiction, he says. ...<br />
<br />
Some writers, for instance, consider it acceptable to "compress" two or more situations into a single scene -- this even though combining two conversations into one was a key element in the famous 1980s libel case against The New Yorker's <a href="http://archive.salon.com/people/bc/2000/02/29/malcolm/index.html">Janet Malcolm</a>, over her profile of controversial psychoanalyst Jeffrey Masson.
