<a href="http://gangrey.com/855">
<h2 class="design">Tea Town</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">We are what we drink?</h4>

<p>

Kruse with a <a href="http://www.sptimes.com/2007/03/26/Hernando/Does_McDonald_s_have_.shtml">study</a> of sweet tea geography trends: McDonald's has been selling its so-labeled and specifically pitched product called Mickey D's Sweet Tea for a while now in select markets on the East Coast and particularly in the South. Here's what's interesting about this: Hernando County has not been one of those markets.<br />
<br />
Mickey D's Sweet Tea is sold in Ocala. It's sold in Orlando. It's sold in Perry and in the Panhandle and even in Naples and New Port Richey.<br />
<br />
But not here.<br />
<br />
Here, then, in the form of a drink offered, or not offered, at McDonald's, is another, potentially meaningful way to get at something of an answer to what has been a longtime question: What is the South and what is the North here in Florida? And here in Hernando?<br />
<br />
What can Mickey D's Sweet Tea teach us about where we live?
