<a href="http://gangrey.com/939">
<h2 class="design">Covering Pasadena From Where?</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">The future</h4>

<p>

This is a <a href="http://www.latimes.com/business/la-fi-pasadena11may11,1,7515978.story">joke</a>, right? <br />
<br />
<i>When is local journalism not really local? When it's about Pasadena and written by someone in India.<br />
<br />
James Macpherson, editor and publisher of the Pasadena Now website, hired two reporters last weekend to cover the Pasadena City Council. One lives in Mumbai and will be paid $12,000 a year. The other will work in Bangalore for $7,200.<br />
<br />
The council broadcasts its meetings on the Web. From nearly 9,000 miles away, the outsourced journalists plan to watch, then write their stories while their boss sleeps &#8212; India is 12.5 hours ahead of Pacific Standard Time.<br />
<br />
"A lot of the routine stuff we do can be done by really talented people in another time zone at much lower wages," said Macpherson, 51, who used to run a clothing business with manufacturing help from Vietnam and India.</i>
