<a href="http://gangrey.com/1039">
<h2 class="design">Buried Treasure</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">A man who likes to dig finds something special in the earth</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.latimes.com/news/nationworld/columnone/la-na-petrifiedtrees28jun28,1,104979.story?coll=la-headlines-columnone&ctrack=1&cset=true">Tomas Alex Tizon</a> (thanks, rlake): <i>Clyde Friend's life changed the moment his bulldozer hit the first tree on a hot summer afternoon in 2002 as he leveled a hill behind his workshop. Chips flew everywhere, a small explosion of brown and white shards. <br />
<br />
He hopped off the dozer to investigate. There, embedded in the hill, was a mostly intact fossilized tree trunk standing upright in solid rock. "Well, that's different," he recalls thinking. <br />
<br />
A heavy-machine operator for most of his working life, Friend was used to finding bits of petrified wood now and then. He'd never bumped into anything like this. <br />
<br />
For the next several days, in the privacy of his remote 10-acre lot, Friend dug up the rest of his find by chipping away the surrounding rock. It turned out to be a petrified hickory tree, 18 feet tall and as big around as a cantaloupe. </i>
