<br />
<i>Shortly after you posted the Las Vegas Sun weather story, I, too, was assigned a weather story. It's getting hot (90 degrees plus a lovely lake breeze is 'getting too hot' here in Erie, Pa), how are people dealing with the heat and lack of rain, how long will it last, etc.   <br />
  <br />
Though I tried to use the Sun story as inspiration, I failed horribly at making anything creative or interesting out of a ho-hum assignment given to many interns and starting reporters. It's something I'm not proud to have my name on, and after it ran, I thought of about 5 things I could have done with it to actually make something out of nothing. I want to know -- how do Gangreyers get inspired to make something out of nothing assignments like these, especially on deadline? I want examples--your best weather story, house fire story, parade story, etc. Anything you did with a bad assignment early in your career that impressed editors? <br />
  <br />
I realize that I may be your only reader who is a lowly summer intern, but maybe not. Who knows. But reading that weather story  made me want to read more like it.</i> <br />
