<a href="http://gangrey.com/1078">
<h2 class="design">A Generation Poisoned</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">WHY MUST YOU PERSECUTE US, J.K. ROWLING?</h4>

<p>

I resisted the lure of Harry Potter for as long as I could, until late 2005, after the sixth book came out. Then my youngest brother, William, who was 15, persuaded me to crack open the first one. I was hooked almost immediately. I read all six books within the next couple of months, and now I&#8217;m nearly through the seventh. I suspect there are others like me in Gangrey Nation.<br />
<br />
We can learn a lot about storytelling from the Potter series, but that&#8217;s not what I want to talk about today. I want to talk about how depicts journalism.<br />
<br />
In general, J.K Rowling seems to hate the press. The main newspaper in her fantasy world, The Daily Prophet, is frequently ignorant of key events, controlled by evil government officials, or flat-out wrong. Rita Skeeter, the newspaper writer, is a bona fide villain. She&#8217;s not just inaccurate &#8211; she&#8217;s maliciously inaccurate. She has a magical device called a Quick-Quotes Quill or something that seems borrowed from Jayson Blair. She is all the bad in all of us, and none of the good.<br />
<br />
Now. About sixty-four gajillion children around the world have read these books. For many of them, this is their introduction to the world of journalism.<br />
<br />
Small wonder nobody reads us anymore. A whole generation sees us as Harry Potter&#8217;s enemies.<br />
<br />
Discuss.<br />

