<a href="http://gangrey.com/1464">
<h2 class="design">Advice</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">From the gangrey mailbag</h4>

<p>

Candace writes: We're working on our big Horizons edition and I'm working on a story on polio victims, this many years later. So, you know, this is a small community &#8212; about 30,000 people in the county, and I know, and am kin to, most of them. So Sunday I was at lunch, and this distant cousin comes up to me and asks if I know Mrs. Devers. I don't. He said, "Well, she's a polio survivor, was diagnosed with it in the late 20s. She spent two years in an iron lung, and another year or so at Warm Springs, Ga. and she got to know FDR pretty well there." Wow.<br />
<br />
You can probably guess what happens next. Mrs. Devers is 87, had polio when she was 7 (late 1920s) &#8212; and I can verify that. In fact, I go to church with her doctor, and he told me she is post-polio. It's probably likely that she was at Warm Springs, and she probably knew FDR. The times on that kind of jive. But almost nothing else does. She told me she was the first poster girl for the March of Dimes (which wasn't founded until the '40s). The iron lung thing is real iffy, and it doesn't seem possible that she could have been in a mobile iron lung taken from town to town in a freak show, like she said, given the electricity coverage in the south in the 1920s. But now, everybody's talking about this big story coming out about Mrs. Devers, and about her being the first March of Dimes poster girl, and spending two years in the iron lung. She told me she would be in it about two hours a day &#8212; well that's not how iron lungs work. She told me she could stick out her left hand that wasn't crippled and people would give her money. Iron lungs don't have arm holes, and I don't know about the 20s, but in the '50s no one touched polio victims &#8212; they were too afraid.<br />
<br />
So this should be a slam dunk, I guess. I should just expose this sweet &#8212; terribly sweet cookie baking, sweet smiling grandmother &#8212; as a liar and a fake, but I have to still live here. And frankly, a lot more people are going to believe her than me. She's been telling this story for years. Should I just focus more on the Warm Springs house, quote the things FDR supposedly said to her that are so dear, and how she suffered? Or will the world be a better place if I do my job right? Any thoughts?
