<a href="http://gangrey.com/1475">
<h2 class="design">Dead Ends</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">storytelling with investigation</h4>

<p>

Karyn Spencer has been working on a set of investigative stories out of Nebraska for a good while. The first part ran <a href="http://www.omaha.com/index.php?u_page=2798&u_sid=10201205">Sunday</a>: CHADRON, Neb. - Tara Russell's body left few clues behind.<br />
<br />
Her body rested on its side, her arms bent like a boxer ready to fight, on the living room floor of her trailer.<br />
<br />
Her husband split town when cops kept asking questions - his departure raised a few more.<br />
<br />
Two months later and 850 miles away, Samantha Lezark's cause of death was no mystery.<br />
<br />
She had been murdered, her battered body found on her bedroom floor in Texas.<br />
<br />
Both women were slender brunettes in their late 20s. They had tattoos and attitude.<br />
<br />
They had something else in common:<br />
<br />
Kris Russell.<br />
<br />
Tara's husband was Samantha's new boyfriend.<br />
<br />
Their deaths a little more than five years ago provide a window into how Nebraska investigates deaths.<br />
<br />
With hardly any state requirements, county coroners and local investigators handle cases as they please.<br />
<br />
Authorities who investigated Tara's death went well beyond the minimal standards, with their training and their work on her case.<br />
<br />
But their efforts didn't satisfy the two families whose daughters ended up in morgues.<br />
<br />
Samantha's mother put it this way: "If they had done their jobs, my daughter would still be alive."<br />
<br />
Second part is <a href="http://www.omaha.com/index.php?u_page=2798&u_sid=10201222">here</a>. Check them out. Let Karyn know what you think.
