<br />
The administration&#8217;s communications experts responded swiftly. Early one Friday morning, they put a group of retired military officers on one of the jets normally used by Vice President Dick Cheney and flew them to Cuba for a carefully orchestrated tour of Guant&aacute;namo.<br />
<br />
To the public, these men are members of a familiar fraternity, presented tens of thousands of times on television and radio as &#8220;military analysts&#8221; whose long service has equipped them to give authoritative and unfettered judgments about the most pressing issues of the post-Sept. 11 world. <br />
<br />
Hidden behind that appearance of objectivity, though, is a Pentagon information apparatus that has used those analysts in a campaign to generate favorable news coverage of the administration&#8217;s wartime performance, an examination by The New York Times has found.
