<a href="http://gangrey.com/1763">
<h2 class="design">Change On The Plains</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">bearing celestial witness</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.nytimes.com/2008/08/04/us/04land.html?_r=1&ref=us&oref=slogin">Dan Barry</a>: AINSWORTH, Neb.<br />
<br />
Driving south out of the agricultural town of Ainsworth, you can&#8217;t miss its newest crop: wind turbines, three dozen of them, with steel stalks 230 feet high and petal-like blades 131 feet long, sprouting improbably from the sand hills of north-central Nebraska, beside ruminating cattle.<br />
<br />
Though painted gray, the turbines stand out against the evening backdrop of battleship-colored thunderclouds and bear an almost celestial whiteness when day&#8217;s light is right. Airplane pilots can spot them from far away, and rarely does a bird make their unfortunate acquaintance.<br />
<br />
The sound of 8.5-ton blades, three to a turbine, turning and turning, only enhances their almost supernatural presence. Standing at the base of a turbine&#8217;s stalk, you hear a whistling whoosh &#8212; whuh ... whuh ... whuh &#8212; as steady summer winds come like the breath of gods to toy with pinwheel amusements.
