<a href="http://gangrey.com/1979">
<h2 class="design">Scooped Again</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">IS IT THE CULTURE?</h4>

<p>

Newsweek (thanks, Nigel) has this <a href="http://www.newsweek.com/id/183028/output/print">fascinating account</a> of how a reporter at Portland's alt-weekly scooped the <i>Oregonian</i> on yet another huge story. Near the bottom is a strange quote from Poynter's own Kelly McBride:<br />
<br />
"I know from talking to people at The Oregonian that they take very seriously their capacity to break these kinds of stories, and there's a lot of hand-wringing when somebody beats them," McBride says. "But it makes perfect sense to me that they would get scooped, because their culture is so tilted toward the narrative form of storytelling."<br />
<br />
***<br />
<br />
This rings false to me. Poynter's own <i>St. Petersburg Times</i> is as well-known for narrative as any other newspaper, but it makes a living on hard-hitting scoops. That newsroom is full of investigative bulldogs who would love nothing more than to break a big scandal.<br />
<br />
Could a penchant for storytelling really diminish a paper's killer instinct?
