<a href="http://gangrey.com/1988">
<h2 class="design">Her Stories</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">coming undone</h4>

<p>

Sorry for so few posts this week. I'm in Tulsa, in St. Francis hospital, room 2316, with my granny, Cledith Montgomery, the best storyteller I've ever known.<br />
<br />
My fondest memories involve snuggling up beside her on the porch swing at her farm in Slick, Okla., and listening to her talk about the time she dropped a wrench from a treehouse and almost killed her little sister, or the time she tried to learn to drive and ran into the side of the barn and blamed it on a wasp, or the time her boyfriend tried to teach her to swim and nearly let her drown in the Little Deep Fork.<br />
<br />
Or the time a horse kicked my brother Matt in the head. <br />
<br />
Or the time grampa drove her through an ice storm to this very hospital, on January 12, 1978, to hold her newest grandson.<br />
<br />
She had a stroke on Saturday morning. Grampa found her on the bathroom floor.<br />
<br />
Last night she was alert, so when the vistors left and the hospital got quiet, I tried to coax some stories out of her.<br />
<br />
I asked her if she remembered the one about the old dog stealing the slice of pie off her plate. (She flung her fork at the retreating dog, the story goes, and it stuck in his nose.)<br />
<br />
"Oh, yes," she said. <br />
<br />
I grabbed my notebook. <br />
<br />
"We never put a lot of anything on a dog, but that dog didn't know how to eat because we never let him."<br />
<br />
She knows she's confused. You can see her frustration. But she still wants to tell stories.<br />
<br />
"Oh, did we ever tell you the one where we ran over the old sack? The old dog ran up and filled up his space with poop, mine and his and his, and that old dog ran off and I killed three of those hogs, and I cried and I cried and I cried. That old red, droopy lima bean I ate, she didn't like me but she sure was mad. If you could see what he's been doing. He dug up a whole lot of stainless steel. I don't know what daddy was giving us in our plate lunches, but when he came out that little dog of ours had grabbed one of those little pigs and had eaten all of it, and boy daddy was mad, and he put that load under the car and off he went to town, and boy, oh boy, was it a nice town."<br />
<br />
Then she fell asleep.<br />
<br />
She woke a few more times. She'd talk a little , or sing a few lines from an old country song called "I'll Meet You In The Morning," then fall asleep.<br />
<br />
Once, when she woke, she looked over at me.<br />
<br />
"Isn't it funny how you remember it all so easy?" she said. "But it's just too much to keep it all in your paint can."<br />
<br />
It's hard to watch granny fade. It's harder to watch her stories fall apart. <br />
<br />
I find comfort in the fact that I can keep them a little longer in my paint can.
