<a href="http://gangrey.com/2239">
<h2 class="design">Poppy's Story</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">face of addiction</h4>

<p>

Paul Grondahl with Poppy's Story in <a href="http://www.timesunion.com/specialreports/poppy/">seven chapters</a> (thanks, Brendan). Here's <a href="http://www.timesunion.com/AspStories/story.asp?storyID=855850">the first</a>: ALBANY -- "I ain't nobody, boss. I ain't nobody."<br />
<br />
The man was wrapped in three layers of thermal wear: coveralls, snowmobile suit, down jacket. He wore a wool hat with earflaps. <br />
<br />
He had come to a South End food pantry for a free lunch on a Saturday in January. Outside, it was wicked cold. A biting wind knifed up from the Hudson a few blocks away -- lip-chapping weather. <br />
<br />
He'd spent the night outside, he said, and had been living on the streets, more on than off, for eight years. Right now, home was a blue-tarp shanty near the 787 overpass, down by the river. <br />
<br />
"Wanna see it?" he asked. He leaned in close, gauging my reaction. <br />
<br />
He smelled of damp earth and stale beer. <br />
<br />
Before I could reply, he requested a cup of tea and off I went to fetch it. He wasn't drinking the watery coffee poured from the spigot of a big silver urn like the others at the pantry. <br />
<br />
My daughter had come to the pantry with a friend as part of a middle school community service project. I joined them in table-waiting duties. <br />
<br />
