<a href="http://gangrey.com/2260">
<h2 class="design">What The Witnesses Saw</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">of the deer's demise</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2009/11/09/AR2009110903090.html">John Kelly</a>: <i>A deer that jumped a wall at the National Zoo was fatally injured by two lions Sunday as dozens of startled spectators looked on. <br />
<br />
-- The Washington Post, Nov. 9 </i><br />
<br />
Did I see it? Of course I saw it. Very disturbing, sir. Very disturbing. The lions' technique was all wrong. Paws when they should have used jaws; jaws when they should have used paws. <br />
<br />
And did you see when one of the lions had the deer in her clutches and let it get away? That showed a lack of focus. She should have bitten the creature's neck, compressing the windpipe. But instead she looked away. Distracted by the crowd? Perhaps. But as I always say, when you are on the hunt, you cannot afford to be distracted. Eye of the tiger, my friend. Eye of the tiger. <br />
<br />
All in all, not a great day for the great cats, although I must note that this episode proves my point that lions are overrated. King of the jungle indeed. Overfed bureaucrat of the jungle is more like it. <br />
<br />
<i>-- Tiger, one cage over </i>
