<h4 class="byline">Cut. Stack. Burn. </h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.statesman.com/news/content/news/stories/local/2009/11/22/1122bonfire.html?imw=Y">Kevin Robbins</a>: COLLEGE STATION &#8212; Long tables draped with maroon linens held the portraits of the dead. Other photographs were displayed Tuesday at G. Rollie White Coliseum, including images depicting the progression of the Texas A&amp;M University sacrament known as Bonfire: cut, stack, burn. One section of pictures portrayed the events of Nov. 18, 1999. <br />
<br />
But John Comstock was drawn to the portraits in the black frames. He gazed at the eyes looking back. <br />
<br />
"Hi, John." <br />
<br />
Comstock turned. <br />
<br />
"Darrin Allen. I found you in the stack." <br />
<br />
Comstock searched the man for signs. The eyes. They were blue. Did Comstock recognize those eyes from that morning in 1999? Maybe he did. <br />
<br />
People change in 10 years. Comstock was a dark-haired and mischievous 19-year-old freshman who liked being around people &#8212; at parties, on campus, at Bonfire &#8212; in November of that year. He still had his left leg. His right arm worked. So did his right foot. He could walk and swing an axe. At stack he wore a bandana under a black military surplus helmet &#8212; Aggies call them "pots" &#8212; indicating his residence at Moses Hall. <br />
<br />
The last time Comstock used his pot was back then, when the 90-year-old tradition of burning six tiers of timber before the annual football game with Texas also changed forever. 
