<a href="http://gangrey.com/2369">
<h2 class="design">Justice And Friendship</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">C, For Convicted</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-judge-friendship28-2010feb28,0,435910.story">Kurt Streeter</a> (thanks, Richard): The man, a thin and gray-haired federal judge, walked nervously up and down the streets of skid row, past drug dealers, pimps and thugs, past rows of men lying like glass-eyed zombies against concrete walls.<br />
<br />
"Excuse me," he said, pulling out a photograph, "have you seen this man?" He was met by blank faces or angry stares. And, always, one word: "No."<br />
<br />
He couldn't give up. Down more streets and through urine-soaked alleys. He was the only white person he could see.<br />
<br />
To Judge Spencer Letts, then 72, this distinction did not matter. What mattered was that Michael Banyard, an ex-con who had lived much of his adult life in prison, could be in trouble again. 
