<br />
White dust choked the air. Ghost-gray figures stalked about, digging at mountains of shattered concrete.<br />
<br />
Robenson Jacques, 33, sprinted up the hill to his house on Rue Estime. The bottom-floor office where his father sold lottery tickets had crumpled like a can.<br />
<br />
Sony's dead," his mom told him.<br />
<br />
"Mama, please don't say that! Sony cannot be dead."<br />
<br />
"He is," she said, quietly sobbing.<br />
<br />
Robenson climbed over the ruins of his neighbors' dwellings to the back of the house. He rushed from person to person, begging: "Have you seen my father? Is he inside? Has anyone seen Sony?"<br />
<br />
He could barely breathe. His mind kept flashing to a single thought: But I haven't done anything for him yet.
