<h4 class="byline">riffing</h4>

<p>

We were talking here the other day about how much we enjoy well-done ruminations on ordinary items after Kruse dropped <a href="http://www.tampabay.com/news/business/retail/in-the-dark-of-holiday-retail-a-scented-candle-lights-the-way/1136687">this ditty about Yankee Candles</a> in the Metro section.<br />
<br />
I tried a while back to give <a href="http://www.tampabay.com/features/popculture/article493141.ece">red plastic cups</a> their due. Taquitos, too. I'm so very fond of <a href="http://books.google.com/books?id=QlqerjJTgU8C&lpg=PA131&ots=wnUxWKDlxx&dq=%22hank%20stuever%22%20and%20patio%20chairs&pg=PA130#v=onepage&q&f=false">Stuever's story about uncelebrated resin</a>:<br />
<br />
<i>There's something about the plastic patio chair.<br />
No, there's not.<br />
And that's what it is about them.</i><br />
<br />
Henry Allen is genius at this. If you've never read his story about <a href="http://articles.latimes.com/1990-06-18/news/vw-256_1_crayon-boxes">Crayons</a> (partial), please do. It's in Nexis.<br />
<br />
Anyhow, Kelley got the great idea to start a collection. Let's help it grow. What are your favorites?
