<h4 class="byline">determined to raise its collective hand</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.nytimes.com/2010/12/08/us/08land.html?pagewanted=1&_r=1&hp">Dan Barry</a>: HOOPER, Neb. &#8212; A few years ago, the Nebraska Department of Roads rolled out a highway bypass to hasten the already-hurried everyday pace. Motorists rushing north to Norfolk, or south to Omaha, no longer had to slow to 40 miles an hour through a blink-and-miss-it place called Hooper. <br />
<br />
No longer did travelers have to pass the Hooper ice cream parlor, or the Hooper grain elevator, or the ancient railroad cars sitting on discontinued tracks, or the decades-old neon marquee, long past glowing, that welcomed travelers to a downtown from the late 19th century. <br />
<br />
The people of Hooper &#8212; population 827, more or less &#8212; knew what this meant. The small green sign planted beside the new highway barely whispered their town&#8217;s name. And in the flat terrain of rural Nebraska, the eye can see far into the distance, yet miss so much. They feared being missed. 
