<h2 class="design">Coming To Terms</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">'I want you to know that I already forgive you.'</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-bo-20101215,0,5294494,print.story">Joe Mozingo</a> (thanks, Mark): He spent much of his life consumed by what the three men on the screen before him had done.<br />
<br />
He stared at the glossy, bloodshot eyes of the man in the middle, the one who had so casually demonstrated how he slit his victims' throats, who explained how his hand grew so sore he often switched to stabbing them at the base of the neck.<br />
<br />
They were gaunt figures now, impoverished men trudging the rice ponds of northwestern Cambodia. They had agreed to confess their roles in the Killing Fields, first for a documentary film, "Enemies of the People," and then here, in a video conference with survivors in Long Beach.<br />
<br />
Bo Uce, 39, listened to them explain that they had to obey orders or they too would be executed. He knew they would say this, and they were right. But it didn't matter.
