<br />
Eric was scared, confused. He&#8217;d just had a terrible dream: That he died too young. That he left behind a wife and five children, and so many unfinished things. And that he needed to do something about it before it was too late.<br />
<br />
He got out of bed. He crept past the boys&#8217; bedrooms, down both sets of stairs of their split-level home in Ankeny, and into the basement toy room. He was surrounded by Legos, board games, cars, trucks, a plastic kitchen. His blond hair, or what was left of it after 31 years, was askew.<br />
<br />
Then Eric Jacobs &#8212; a father who devoted every Sunday to family day, an evangelist who&#8217;d handed over his soul to Jesus Christ, a man whose life was filled with joy and promise &#8212; turned on the lights, sat on the floor next to the furnace closet, looked into a camera mounted on a Dell laptop, and clicked record.
